## Proof

**Key Lemma.** For all $n \ge 0$ and $k \ge 1$,
$$a_{n+k} \equiv a_{k-1} \pmod{a_n}.$$

*Proof by induction on $k$.* 

Base case $k=1$: $a_{n+1} = a_n^2 + 1 \equiv 1 = a_0 = a_{k-1} \pmod{a_n}$. ✓

Inductive step: Suppose $a_{n+k} \equiv a_{k-1} \pmod{a_n}$. Then
$$a_{n+k+1} = a_{n+k}^2 + 1 \equiv a_{k-1}^2 + 1 = a_k \pmod{a_n}. \quad \checkmark$$

**Corollary (Reduction).** For $m \ge n+1$,
$$\gcd(a_m, a_n) = \gcd(a_n,\, a_{m-n-1}),$$
since $a_m \equiv a_{m-n-1} \pmod{a_n}$.

---

**Computation of $\gcd(a_{2019}, a_{999})$.**

Apply the reduction with $m = 2019,\, n = 999$:
$$\gcd(a_{2019}, a_{999}) = \gcd(a_{999},\, a_{2019-999-1}) = \gcd(a_{999},\, a_{1019}).$$

Apply again with $m = 1019,\, n = 999$:
$$\gcd(a_{999}, a_{1019}) = \gcd(a_{999},\, a_{1019-999-1}) = \gcd(a_{999},\, a_{19}).$$

Now repeatedly apply the reduction with $n = 19$ (each step replaces the larger index $m$ by $m - 19 - 1 = m - 20$):

$$\gcd(a_{999}, a_{19}) = \gcd(a_{19}, a_{979}) = \gcd(a_{979}, a_{19}) = \gcd(a_{19}, a_{959}) = \cdots$$

The first index descends through $999, 979, 959, \ldots$ subtracting $20$ each time. Since
$$999 = 19 + 49 \cdot 20,$$
after $49$ reductions we reach
$$\gcd(a_{39}, a_{19}) = \gcd(a_{19}, a_{39-19-1}) = \gcd(a_{19}, a_{19}) = a_{19}.$$

---

**Verification of the arithmetic.** $2019 - 999 - 1 = 1019$; $\ 1019 - 999 - 1 = 19$; $\ 999 - 19 = 980 = 49 \times 20$, so the index $999$ reduces to $19$ in exactly $49$ steps of subtracting $20$.

---

**Conclusion.**

$$\gcd(a_{999},\, a_{2019}) = a_{19}.$$

